# Space-source upstream revision

What implementing a reviewed space unit proves wrong in that design or its inherited site and settings interfaces, and what is repaired there. Every selected space-source export answers for the space unit it realizes.

## Design revision from space-source work {#design-revision-from-space-source-work}

Implementation tests whether space design fixes constructible boundaries, openings, containment, adjacency, clear dimensions, routes, contacts, and observation geometry. Report any topology, transform, clearance, portal, or site-interface decision the export could not realize without inventing it. Repair the space, map, or settings owner before changing source; hidden collision geometry or a convenient opening is not an upstream repair.

`@evidence` names every upstream target repaired and the implementation case that exposed it. When the export exposes no defect, `@evidenceExclude` names its space parent and the concrete boundaries, dimensions, routes, contacts, and interfaces implemented as written. A parentless export or generic build-success claim covers nothing.

Review question: what did implementing this space-source export prove wrong in its actual space or earlier parents, or which concrete parent decisions did it test and find sufficient?

Sources: [buildingSMART on space boundaries and related building elements](https://standards.buildingsmart.org/IFC/RELEASE/IFC4_3/HTML/lexical/IfcRelSpaceBoundary.htm); [NASA on interface verification and traceability](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/systems-engineering-handbook/)
